From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: michael moore <mrmoore27@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 07:15:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/27] prime: catch the hybrid proxy with shared canaries and
 decoys
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A gateway that runs the registered node and merges its mempool into
the pool's template carries its own canaries, so own canaries alone
passed it (A2 residual #1). Two new canary kinds cover every gateway
at once:

- shared: in the pool node's mempool (relayed on its base fee) but
  never in its template. An honest node templates it. A hybrid that
  follows the pool's exclusions misses it.
- decoy: zero fee, prioritised on the pool node only, so it is in the
  pool's template and no honest node ever has it. A hybrid that
  merges into the pool's template carries it.

Own canaries force a hybrid to take its node's side of a conflict.
Decoys force T ⊆ L, and shared canaries force T ⊇ L. A template that
passes all three is its own node's tx set.

Flagged when a must-carry class (own or shared, >= 10 due) is carried
< 25 % of the time, or when >= 25 % of due decoys are carried. Decoys
convict but never clear. A flagged gateway's independence is now 0
(it used to keep hits/due as its bonus weight). The rows publish
flag_reasons. POST /sovereignty/canary takes an optional kind.

Local research branch rnd/sov-004 (SOV-004); never pushed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
